<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Skeletal (fill-in) version of 3.4 The Cross Product.

  Definitions, theorems, summaries, and problem statements are kept;
  derivations and solutions are replaced by blank workspace boxes, each
  with a one-line statement of what has to be done.  Content mirrors
  the-cross-product.ptx of the full lecture notes; every xml:id here is
  prefixed with "skel-" so the two can coexist.

  Figures: every figure in this section sets a problem up rather than
  answering one (the parallelogram, the two right-hand-rule photographs,
  the two torque groupings, the biceps diagram, the moving polar frame,
  the chord, the sign of omega, and omega cross r), so all of them are
  reproduced in full, as in the lecture notes, together with the videos
  and the torque interactive.  The two true/false exercises keep their
  statements; their worked feedback becomes a workspace to justify the
  answer in.
-->
<section xml:id="sec-skel-geom-cross-product" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>The Cross Product</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Understand the cross product as an operation that produces a vector
perpendicular to two given vectors, with its direction fixed by the
right-hand rule.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Interpret the magnitude of the cross product as the area of the parallelogram
spanned by the two vectors, and recognize that the cross product is zero
exactly when the vectors are parallel.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Understand why the geometric and component definitions of the cross product
describe one and the same object.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Contrast the cross product with the dot product: one returns a vector and
detects parallelism, while the other returns a scalar and detects
perpendicularity.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Understand torque as a measure of the turning effect of a force, and
appreciate the role of cross product in determining torque.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Describe circular motion with the moving unit vectors <m>\hat r</m> and
<m>\hat\theta</m>, and represent the rate of rotation by the angular
velocity vector <m>\overrightarrow{\omega}</m> along the axis of rotation.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Use the cross product to obtain the velocity of a rotating object from its
angular velocity and position, <m>\overrightarrow{v} = \overrightarrow{\omega} \times \overrightarrow{r}</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-cross-definition">
<title>Definition and Properties of the Cross Product</title>

<definition xml:id="def-skel-cross-product">
<title>The Cross Product (First Definition)</title>
<statement>
<p>
Given two vectors <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m> in
<m>\mathbb R^3</m>, the <term>cross product</term>
<m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf v</m> is the <em>vector</em> defined by
<md xml:id="eq-skel-cross-geometric" number="yes">
\mathbf u \times \mathbf v = \left(\|\mathbf u\|\|\mathbf v\|\sin\theta\right)\hat{n},
</md>
where <m>\theta</m>, with <m>0 \le \theta \le \pi</m>, is the angle
between <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m>, and <m>\hat{n}</m> is
the unit vector orthogonal to both <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m>
whose direction is given by the right-hand rule.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
The magnitude of <m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf v</m> is equal to the area
of the parallelogram built on the two vectors <m>\mathbf u</m> and
<m>\mathbf v</m>, as shown in <xref ref="fig-skel-parallelogram-area"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-parallelogram-area">
<caption>
The parallelogram built on the vectors <m>\mathbf u</m> and
<m>\mathbf v</m>, with base <m>\|\mathbf v\|</m> and height
<m>\|\mathbf u\||\sin(\theta)|</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-parallelogram-area">
<diagram dimensions="(381,200)" margins="10">
<definition>u=(2,3)</definition>
<definition>v=(5,0)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-0.6,7.5,3.6)">
<line at="par-side-top" endpoints="(u, u+v)" stroke="red" thickness="2"/>
<line at="par-side-right" endpoints="(v, u+v)" stroke="blue" thickness="2"/>
<line at="par-height" endpoints="(u, (2,0))" stroke="black" dash="4 4"/>
<vector at="par-vec-u" v="u" tail="(0,0)" stroke="blue"/>
<vector at="par-vec-v" v="v" tail="(0,0)" stroke="red"/>
<label at="par-u-label" anchor="(1,1.5)" alignment="nw" color="blue">
<m>\mathbf u</m>
</label>
<label at="par-v-label" anchor="(2.3,-0.2)" alignment="s" color="red">
<m>\mathbf v</m>
</label>
<label at="par-height-label" anchor="(2.1,1.5)" alignment="e">
<m>\|\mathbf u\|\sin(\theta)</m>
</label>
<angle-marker at="par-angle-theta" points="(v,(0,0),u)" radius="20" clear-background="no">
<m>\theta</m>
</angle-marker>
</coordinates>
<annotations text="A parallelogram built on the vectors u and v, drawn tail to tail from the origin, with v along the horizontal direction. A dashed vertical segment from the tip of u down to v marks the height of the parallelogram, equal to the length of u times the sine of theta.">
<annotation ref="par-vec-u" text="Vector u, drawn from the origin at angle theta above v."/>
<annotation ref="par-vec-v" text="Vector v, along the horizontal direction."/>
<annotation ref="par-side-top" text="The side of the parallelogram parallel to v, starting at the tip of u."/>
<annotation ref="par-side-right" text="The side of the parallelogram parallel to u, starting at the tip of v."/>
<annotation ref="par-height" text="The dashed height of the parallelogram, with length equal to the length of u times the sine of theta."/>
<annotation ref="par-angle-theta" text="The angle theta between u and v, located at the origin."/>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Read the area of that parallelogram off the figure, and compare it with
<xref ref="eq-skel-cross-geometric"/>. (Use base times height, with the base
<m>\|\mathbf v\|</m> and the height marked in the picture. Say why an absolute
value is needed on <m>\sin\theta</m> even though
<xref ref="def-skel-cross-product"/> does not carry one.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {Area $=$ base $\times$ height $=$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {Why the absolute value:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the area of the parallelogram as base times height and one for the reason the absolute value of the sine is needed.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<fact xml:id="fact-skel-parallelogram-area">
<title>Area of the Parallelogram</title>
<statement>
<p>
<md xml:id="eq-skel-parallelogram-area" number="yes">
\text{Area of the parallelogram}
= \|\mathbf u\|\|\mathbf v\||\sin\theta|
= \|\mathbf u \times \mathbf v\|.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</fact>

<p>
The vector <m>\hat{n}</m> is the unit vector that determines the
direction of <m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf v</m> using the right-hand
rule. Note that <m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf v</m> is orthogonal
(perpendicular) to both vectors, as shown in
<xref ref="fig-skel-cross-product-video"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-cross-product-video">
<caption>
The cross product <m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf v</m> points in the
direction of the unit vector <m>\hat{n}</m>, given by the right-hand
rule, and is orthogonal to both <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m>;
its length is <m>\|\mathbf u\|\|\mathbf v\||\sin\theta|</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-cross-product"
       source="videos/cross_product_3d_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-right-hand-rule-illustration">
  <caption>Two equivalent right-hand rules for <m>\mathbf{u}\times\mathbf{v}</m>.</caption>
  <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="2%">
    <figure xml:id="fig-skel-rhr-imt">
      <caption>Index<ndash/>Middle<ndash/>Thumb method.</caption>
      <image source="images/rhr-index-middle-thumb.png" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>Index finger along u, middle finger along v, thumb along u cross v.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <figure xml:id="fig-skel-rhr-curl">
      <caption>Rotating-fingers method.</caption>
      <image source="images/rhr-rotating-fingers.png" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>Fingers curl from u toward v, thumb points along u cross v.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </sidebyside>
</figure>

<fact xml:id="fact-skel-parallel-vectors">
<title>Parallel Vectors</title>
<statement>
<p>
From <xref ref="eq-skel-cross-geometric"/>, it follows that the cross product
of two parallel vectors is zero.
</p>
<p>
<md xml:id="eq-skel-parallel-vectors" number="yes">
\mathbf u \parallel \mathbf v \iff \mathbf u \times \mathbf v = \mathbf 0,
\qquad \mathbf u, \mathbf v \neq \mathbf 0.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</fact>

<p>
Say why <xref ref="eq-skel-parallel-vectors"/> follows, in one line. (Which
factor of <xref ref="eq-skel-cross-geometric"/> vanishes, and for which values
of <m>\theta</m>? Remember that parallel covers both the same and the opposite
direction, and note that this is the cross product's counterpart of
<xref ref="eq-skel-orthogonal"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,5);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,4.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for explaining why the cross product of two parallel vectors is the zero vector.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<theorem xml:id="thm-skel-cross-properties">
<title>Properties of the Cross Product</title>
<statement>
<p>
If <m>\mathbf u</m>, <m>\mathbf v</m>, and <m>\mathbf w</m> are any
vectors and <m>r</m>, <m>s</m> are scalars, then
</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li><p><m>(r\mathbf u) \times (s\mathbf v) = (rs)(\mathbf u \times \mathbf v)</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>\mathbf u \times (\mathbf v + \mathbf w) = \mathbf u \times \mathbf v + \mathbf u \times \mathbf w</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>(\mathbf v + \mathbf w) \times \mathbf u = \mathbf v \times \mathbf u + \mathbf w \times \mathbf u</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>\mathbf v \times \mathbf u = -(\mathbf u \times \mathbf v)</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>\mathbf 0 \times \mathbf u = \mathbf 0</m></p></li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
Property 4 is the one that has no analogue for the dot product, so prove it.
(Do not use the geometric definition; use the component formula
<xref ref="eq-skel-cross-components"/> from the next subsection, and compare
the first components of the two sides. Then state, in words, what property 4
says about the order of the two factors.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {First components of $\mathbf v\times\mathbf u$ and $\mathbf u\times\mathbf v$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {In words:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the comparison of the first components of the two cross products and one for the statement in words.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<exercise xml:id="skel-exercise-cross-identity">
<statement>
<p>
Show that
<m>(\mathbf u - \mathbf v) \times (\mathbf u + \mathbf v) = 2(\mathbf u \times \mathbf v)</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Expand with the two distributive properties of
<xref ref="thm-skel-cross-properties"/>, which gives four terms. Two of them
vanish by <xref ref="fact-skel-parallel-vectors"/>, and the other two combine
by property 4 <mdash/> mind the double negative.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5.5) -- (15,5.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.7) {Expanding:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.2) {Simplifying:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the four-term expansion of the product and one for its simplification.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</exercise>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-cross-geometric">
<title>Computing a cross product using the first definition</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use <xref ref="def-skel-cross-product"/> to compute
</p>
<p>
<ol>
<li><p><m>\hat{\imath} \times \hat{\jmath}</m>, and also <m>\hat{\jmath} \times \hat{\imath}</m>;</p></li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf v</m>, where <m>\mathbf u = 4\hat{\imath}</m> and
<m>\mathbf v</m> is a vector of length <m>3</m> lying in the
<m>xy</m>-plane, making an angle of <m>30^\circ</m> with <m>\mathbf u</m>
measured counterclockwise.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(In both parts the two vectors lie in the <m>xy</m>-plane, so <m>\hat n</m> can
only be <m>\hat k</m> or <m>-\hat k</m>; the right-hand rule of
<xref ref="fig-skel-right-hand-rule-illustration"/> decides which. Take the
lengths and the angle from the statement, and check that part 1 agrees with
property 4 of <xref ref="thm-skel-cross-properties"/>. Finish by checking the
magnitude in part 2 against <xref ref="eq-skel-parallelogram-area"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10.5) -- (15,10.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.7) {1. $\hat\imath\times\hat\jmath =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.2) {\phantom{1. }$\hat\jmath\times\hat\imath =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {2. $\mathbf u\times\mathbf v =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {Area check:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for i cross j, for j cross i, for the cross product in part two, and for the check against the area of the parallelogram.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-cross-components">
<title>Second Definition of the Cross Product</title>

<p>
Writing the vectors in components gives another definition for the cross
product.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-skel-cross-components">
<title>The Cross Product (Second Definition)</title>
<statement>
<p>
If <m>\mathbf u = \langle u_1, u_2, u_3\rangle</m> and
<m>\mathbf v = \langle v_1, v_2, v_3\rangle</m>, then
<md xml:id="eq-skel-cross-components" number="yes">
\mathbf u \times \mathbf v =
\langle u_2v_3 - u_3v_2,\; u_3v_1 - u_1v_3,\; u_1v_2 - u_2v_1\rangle.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
One way to remember the second definition of the cross product is to
write it as a determinant:
<md>
<mrow>
\mathbf u \times \mathbf v \amp =
\begin{vmatrix}
\hat{\imath} \amp \hat{\jmath} \amp \hat{k} \\
u_1 \amp u_2 \amp u_3 \\
v_1 \amp v_2 \amp v_3
\end{vmatrix}
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-skel-cross-determinant" number="yes">
\amp =
\begin{vmatrix} u_2 \amp u_3 \\ v_2 \amp v_3 \end{vmatrix}\hat{\imath}
- \begin{vmatrix} u_1 \amp u_3 \\ v_1 \amp v_3 \end{vmatrix}\hat{\jmath}
+ \begin{vmatrix} u_1 \amp u_2 \\ v_1 \amp v_2 \end{vmatrix}\hat{k},
</mrow>
</md>
where
<md xml:id="eq-skel-two-by-two-determinant" number="yes">
\begin{vmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{vmatrix} = ad - bc.
</md>
</p>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-cross-compute">
<title>Computing a cross product</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the cross product <m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf v</m> if
<m>\mathbf u = \langle 1,-1,2\rangle</m> and
<m>\mathbf v = \langle 3,-2,1\rangle</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Set up the determinant of <xref ref="eq-skel-cross-determinant"/> with
<m>\mathbf u</m> in the middle row, expand it with
<xref ref="eq-skel-two-by-two-determinant"/>, and mind the minus sign on the
<m>\hat\jmath</m> term. Then check your answer by dotting it with both
<m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m>: by
<xref ref="def-skel-cross-product"/> both results must vanish.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.7) {$\mathbf u\times\mathbf v =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {Check:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the determinant computation of the cross product and one for the orthogonality check.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-perpendicular-vector">
<title>Computing a vector perpendicular to two other vectors</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the points <m>A(1,-1,2)</m>, <m>B(2,0,-1)</m>, and
<m>C(0,-2,3)</m>. Find a unit vector which is orthogonal to both
<m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{AC}</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Build the two vectors with <xref ref="eq-skel-vec-components"/>, cross them,
then normalize with <xref ref="eq-skel-vec-unit"/>. One component of the cross
product comes out zero, which is worth noticing before you take the length.
There are two unit vectors that work; say what the other one is.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,11) -- (15,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5) -- (15,5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,2) -- (15,2);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.7) {$\overrightarrow{AB} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (7.50,13.7) {$\overrightarrow{AC} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.7) {$\overrightarrow{AB}\times\overrightarrow{AC} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.7) {$\hat n =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,1.7) {The other one:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the two vectors, for their cross product, for the unit normal vector, and for the second unit normal vector.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-normal-vector-video">
<caption>
The unit vector <m>\hat{n}</m> is orthogonal to both
<m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{AC}</m>, and hence to the plane through the
points <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-normal-vector"
       source="videos/normal_vector_3d_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
</statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-triangle-area">
<title>Computing the area of a triangle</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the area of the triangle with the vertices <m>A(1,-1,2)</m>,
<m>B(2,0,-1)</m>, and <m>C(0,-2,3)</m>. Note that these are the same
points that we had in the previous example.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-triangle-area">
<caption>
The triangle built on the vectors <m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{AC}</m>
is half of the corresponding parallelogram.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-triangle-area">
<diagram dimensions="(310,200)" margins="10">
<definition>b=(4,0)</definition>
<definition>c=(1.5,3)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-0.6,6,3.6)">
<line at="tri-side-bc" endpoints="(c, b)" stroke="black" dash="4 4"/>
<line at="tri-side-top" endpoints="(c, b+c)" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
<line at="tri-side-right" endpoints="(b, b+c)" stroke="blue" dash="4 4"/>
<vector at="tri-vec-ac" v="c" tail="(0,0)" stroke="blue"/>
<vector at="tri-vec-ab" v="b" tail="(0,0)" stroke="red"/>
<label at="tri-ac-label" anchor="(0.75,1.5)" alignment="nw" color="blue">
<m>\overrightarrow{AC}</m>
</label>
<label at="tri-ab-label" anchor="(2,0)" alignment="s" color="red">
<m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations text="A triangle formed by the vectors AB and AC drawn tail to tail from the vertex A, together with a dashed segment from the tip of AC to the tip of AB. Two more dashed segments complete the parallelogram built on AB and AC, showing that the triangle is half of the parallelogram.">
<annotation ref="tri-vec-ab" text="Vector AB, along the horizontal direction."/>
<annotation ref="tri-vec-ac" text="Vector AC, drawn from the same tail as AB."/>
<annotation ref="tri-side-bc" text="The dashed third side of the triangle, from the tip of AC to the tip of AB."/>
<annotation ref="tri-side-top" text="A dashed side completing the parallelogram, parallel to AB."/>
<annotation ref="tri-side-right" text="A dashed side completing the parallelogram, parallel to AC."/>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(No new cross product is needed <mdash/> reuse the one from
<xref ref="ex-skel-perpendicular-vector"/>. Get the area of the parallelogram
from <xref ref="eq-skel-parallelogram-area"/>, then read off
<xref ref="fig-skel-triangle-area"/> what fraction of it the triangle is.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {Area of the parallelogram $=$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {Area of the triangle $=$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the area of the parallelogram and one for the area of the triangle.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-true-false">
<title>True or False?</title>

<exercise xml:id="skel-exercise-true-false-1">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="yes">
<p>
Let <m>\overrightarrow{u}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{v}</m> be two vectors in
<m>\mathbb R^3</m>. Then, <m>\overrightarrow{u} \times \text{proj}_{\overrightarrow{v}}\overrightarrow{u}</m>
is perpendicular to <m>\overrightarrow{u} - \text{proj}_{\overrightarrow{v}}\overrightarrow{u}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<p>
Justify your answer. (Dot the two vectors together and distribute, using
property 3 of <xref ref="fact-skel-properties"/>. A cross product is
perpendicular to each of its own factors, so both of the resulting terms are
of a form you already know how to evaluate; then appeal to
<xref ref="eq-skel-orthogonal"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,8.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for justifying the first true or false statement.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<exercise xml:id="skel-exercise-true-false-2">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="yes">
<p>
Let <m>\hat{u}</m> and <m>\hat{v}</m> be two standard unit vectors in
<m>\mathbb R^3</m>. Then,
<m>\left(\hat{u} \cdot \hat{v}\right)^2 + \left\|\hat{v} \times \hat{u}\right\|^2 = 1</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<p>
Justify your answer. (Write both terms with the geometric formulas
<xref ref="eq-skel-angle"/> and <xref ref="eq-skel-cross-geometric"/>, so that
each carries a factor of <m>\|\hat u\|^2\|\hat v\|^2</m>. A Pythagorean
identity then collapses what is left. Say which step uses the fact that the
vectors are <em>unit</em> vectors.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,8.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for justifying the second true or false statement.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-torque">
<title>Application: Torque</title>

<p>
One of the well known physical applications of the cross product is
<term>torque</term>, the rotational analog of force. A force makes an
object accelerate; a torque makes it <em>rotate</em>. How effective a
force is at producing rotation depends not only on its magnitude, but
also on <em>where</em> it is applied and in <em>what direction</em>.
Think of pushing a door: pushing hard near the hinge barely moves it,
while a light push at the doorknob swings it open easily. Pushing at the
doorknob but <em>toward</em> the hinge does nothing at all. The cross
product captures all three scenarios and more.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-skel-torque">
<title>Torque</title>
<statement>
<p>
Suppose a force <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> is applied at a point whose position
vector relative to the axis of rotation is <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m>. The
<term>torque</term> produced by <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> is the vector
<md xml:id="eq-skel-torque-def" number="yes">
\overrightarrow{\tau} = \overrightarrow{r} \times \overrightarrow{F}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p component="print">
An interactive view of the torque <m>\overrightarrow{\tau} = \overrightarrow{r} \times \overrightarrow{F}</m> <mdash/> the force swung around the head of <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m>, with the torque vector responding <mdash/> accompanies the online version of these notes.
</p>
<sidebyside component="web" width="100%">
<figure xml:id="fig-skel-int-torque">
<caption>
Interactive view of <xref ref="eq-skel-torque-def"/>. The force
<m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> is applied at the head of <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m>, and the torque
<m>\overrightarrow{\tau} = \overrightarrow{r} \times \overrightarrow{F}</m> points along the axis of rotation.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-skel-torque" iframe="torque-cross-product.html" width="100%" aspect="8:7">
<instructions>
<p>
In the left pane the axis of rotation passes through the marked point,
perpendicular to the screen. Drag the head of the yellow position vector
<m>\overrightarrow{r}</m> to move the point where the force is applied, and drag the
head of the red force <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> to change its size and direction. The
dashed red line is the line along which the force acts, and the violet
segment is the lever arm <m>r_\perp = \|\overrightarrow{r}\|\sin\theta</m>, the
perpendicular distance from the axis to that line. The shaded
parallelogram built on <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> has area
<m>\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\|</m>, and the curved arrow shows the sense of the rotation
the torque produces.
</p>
<p>
The right pane shows the same two vectors in space, together with
<m>\overrightarrow{\tau} = \overrightarrow{r} \times \overrightarrow{F}</m> along the axis; drag that pane to
view it from another angle, or press <q>view</q> to restore the original
one. Press <q>animate</q> to swing <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> once around the point of
application: watch <m>\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\|</m> reach its largest value when
<m>\overrightarrow{F} \perp \overrightarrow{r}</m>, fall to zero when <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> points
straight at (or away from) the axis, and reverse direction as the
rotation changes sense. Either vector can also be moved with the keyboard
(press <q>Tab</q> to reach its head, then use the arrow keys).
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>
</sidebyside>

<p>
Everything we know about the cross product now translates into physics.
By <xref ref="eq-skel-cross-geometric"/>, the magnitude of the torque is
<md xml:id="eq-skel-torque-magnitude" number="yes">
\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\| = \|\overrightarrow{r}\|\|\overrightarrow{F}\||\sin\theta|,
</md>
where <m>\theta</m> is the angle between <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m> and
<m>\overrightarrow{F}</m>, and the direction of <m>\overrightarrow{\tau}</m> is given by the
right-hand rule: <m>\overrightarrow{\tau}</m> points along the axis of rotation, and
curling the fingers of the right hand from <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m> toward
<m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> gives the sense of the rotation. Torque has units of
meter-newtons (<m>\text{m}\cdot\text{N}</m>) in SI.
</p>

<p>
The quantity in <xref ref="eq-skel-torque-magnitude"/> can be grouped in two
useful ways, shown in <xref ref="fig-skel-torque-two-groupings"/>. Write down
the two groupings. (Attach the <m>\sin\theta</m> first to <m>\|\overrightarrow r\|</m>
and then to <m>\|\overrightarrow F\|</m>; each grouping names a quantity in the
figure. Then say why the two products are equal.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4.7) -- (15,4.7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,2.4) -- (15,2.4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {Grouped as a lever arm: $\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\| =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.4) {Grouped as a perpendicular force: $\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\| =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.1) {Why they agree:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the torque grouped as force times lever arm, for the torque grouped as distance times perpendicular force, and for the reason the two agree.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-torque-two-groupings">
<caption>
The two groupings of <xref ref="eq-skel-torque-magnitude"/>. In both pictures
the axis of rotation passes through <m>O</m>, perpendicular to the page, the
force <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> is applied at <m>P</m>, and <m>\theta</m> is the angle
between <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m>; the two products are equal because
each is <m>\|\overrightarrow{r}\|\|\overrightarrow{F}\|\sin\theta</m>.
</caption>
<sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="2%">

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-torque-lever-arm">
<caption>
Force times lever arm: <m>\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\| = r_\perp\|\overrightarrow{F}\|</m>.
</caption>
<image width="100%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-torque-lever-arm">
<diagram dimensions="(330,300)" margins="8">
<coordinates bbox="(-1.1,-3.95,6.5,2.95)">

<line at="la-action" p1="(2.451,-2.212)" p2="(5.721,2.457)"
      stroke="#D55E00" thickness="1.4" dash="5 4"/>
<line at="la-rext" p1="(4,0)" p2="(6.0,0)"
      stroke="#0072B2" thickness="1.2" dash="3 4"/>

<line at="la-lever" endpoints="((0,0), (2.684,-1.879))"
      stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>

<vector at="la-r" v="(4,0)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="la-F" v="(1.491,2.130)" tail="(4,0)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2"/>

<angle-marker at="la-theta" points="((5.6,0),(4,0),(5.491,2.130))"
              radius="30" stroke="black"><m>\theta</m></angle-marker>
<angle-marker at="la-right" points="((5.721,2.457),(2.684,-1.879),(0,0))"
              radius="13" stroke="#009E73"/>

<point at="la-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" radius="3.5"/>
<point at="la-P" p="(4,0)" fill="black" radius="3.5"/>
<point at="la-Q" p="(2.684,-1.879)" fill="#009E73" radius="3"/>

<label at="la-Ol" anchor="(-0.12,-0.10)" alignment="sw"><m>O</m></label>
<label at="la-Pl" anchor="(4.02,0.10)" alignment="nw"><m>P</m></label>
<label at="la-rl" anchor="(2.0,0.08)" alignment="n" color="#0072B2">
<m>\overrightarrow{r}</m>
</label>
<label at="la-Fl" anchor="(5.30,2.35)" alignment="nw" color="#D55E00">
<m>\overrightarrow{F}</m>
</label>
<label at="la-leverl" anchor="(1.22,-0.86)" alignment="ne" color="#009E73">
