</label>
<label at="la-eq1" anchor="(2.75,-3.05)" alignment="c">
<m>r_\perp = \|\overrightarrow{r}\|\sin\theta</m>
</label>
<label at="la-eq2" anchor="(2.75,-3.75)" alignment="c">
<m>\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\| = r_\perp\|\overrightarrow{F}\|</m>
</label>

</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The axis of rotation passes through the point O, perpendicular to the page, and the force F is applied at the point P at the head of the position vector r. The line along which the force acts is extended backwards past P as a dashed line, and it passes below O. The perpendicular segment from O to that line is the lever arm r perpendicular, whose length is the length of r times the sine of theta. The torque is the length of the force times this lever arm.">
  <annotation ref="la-r" text="The position vector r, drawn from the axis at O to the point of application P."/>
  <annotation ref="la-F" text="The force F, drawn from P, making an angle theta with the direction of r."/>
  <annotation ref="la-action" text="The dashed line along which the force acts, extended in both directions past P."/>
  <annotation ref="la-lever" text="The lever arm, the perpendicular segment from the axis at O to the line of action of the force."/>
  <annotation ref="la-right" text="A right angle, showing that the lever arm meets the line of action perpendicularly."/>
  <annotation ref="la-theta" text="The angle theta at P, between the direction of r and the force F."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-torque-perp-force">
<caption>
Distance times perpendicular force:
<m>\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\| = \|\overrightarrow{r}\|F_\perp</m>.
</caption>
<image width="100%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-torque-perp-force">
<diagram dimensions="(330,300)" margins="8">
<coordinates bbox="(-1.1,-3.95,6.5,2.95)">

<line at="fp-boxv" p1="(5.491,0)" p2="(5.491,2.130)"
      stroke="#999999" thickness="1.2" dash="3 4"/>
<line at="fp-boxh" p1="(4,2.130)" p2="(5.491,2.130)"
      stroke="#999999" thickness="1.2" dash="3 4"/>

<vector at="fp-r" v="(4,0)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="fp-Fpar" v="(1.491,0)" tail="(4,0)" stroke="#666666" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="fp-Fperp" v="(0,2.130)" tail="(4,0)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="fp-F" v="(1.491,2.130)" tail="(4,0)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2"/>

<angle-marker at="fp-theta" points="((5.6,0),(4,0),(5.491,2.130))"
              radius="30" stroke="black"><m>\theta</m></angle-marker>
<angle-marker at="fp-right" points="((5.6,0),(4,0),(4,2.130))"
              radius="13" stroke="#009E73"/>

<point at="fp-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" radius="3.5"/>
<point at="fp-P" p="(4,0)" fill="black" radius="3.5"/>

<label at="fp-Ol" anchor="(-0.12,-0.10)" alignment="sw"><m>O</m></label>
<label at="fp-Pl" anchor="(3.94,-0.10)" alignment="sw"><m>P</m></label>
<label at="fp-rl" anchor="(2.0,0.08)" alignment="n" color="#0072B2">
<m>\overrightarrow{r}</m>
</label>
<label at="fp-Fl" anchor="(5.55,2.20)" alignment="ne" color="#D55E00">
<m>\overrightarrow{F}</m>
</label>
<label at="fp-Fperpl" anchor="(3.90,1.15)" alignment="w" color="#009E73">
<m>F_\perp</m>
</label>
<label at="fp-Fparl" anchor="(4.75,-0.12)" alignment="s" color="#666666">
<m>F_\parallel</m>
</label>
<label at="fp-note" anchor="(2.75,-1.95)" alignment="c" color="#666666">
<m>F_\parallel \text{ produces no rotation}</m>
</label>
<label at="fp-eq1" anchor="(2.75,-3.05)" alignment="c">
<m>F_\perp = \|\overrightarrow{F}\|\sin\theta</m>
</label>
<label at="fp-eq2" anchor="(2.75,-3.75)" alignment="c">
<m>\|\overrightarrow{\tau}\| = \|\overrightarrow{r}\|F_\perp</m>
</label>

</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The same position vector r from the axis at O to the point of application P, with the force F at P split into two pieces by a dashed rectangle. One piece is perpendicular to r and has length the length of F times the sine of theta; the other piece is parallel to r and points straight away from the axis. The torque is the length of r times the perpendicular piece; the parallel piece produces no rotation.">
  <annotation ref="fp-r" text="The position vector r, drawn from the axis at O to the point of application P."/>
  <annotation ref="fp-F" text="The force F, drawn from P, making an angle theta with the direction of r."/>
  <annotation ref="fp-Fperp" text="The component of the force perpendicular to r, of length F perpendicular; this is the part that turns the object."/>
  <annotation ref="fp-Fpar" text="The component of the force parallel to r, pointing straight away from the axis; this part produces no rotation."/>
  <annotation ref="fp-right" text="A right angle between the two components of the force."/>
  <annotation ref="fp-theta" text="The angle theta at P, between the direction of r and the force F."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

</sidebyside>
</figure>

<p>
The first grouping, shown in <xref ref="fig-skel-torque-lever-arm"/>, says the
torque is the force times the
<term>lever arm</term> <m>r_\perp</m>, the perpendicular distance from
the axis to the line along which the force acts. The second, shown in
<xref ref="fig-skel-torque-perp-force"/>, says it is
the distance <m>\|\overrightarrow{r}\|</m> times <m>F_\perp</m>, the component of the
force perpendicular to <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m>; the parallel component
<m>F_\parallel</m> points straight at the axis and produces no rotation.
</p>

<fact xml:id="fact-skel-zero-torque">
<title>Zero Torque</title>
<statement>
<p>
By <xref ref="eq-skel-parallel-vectors"/>, if <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> is parallel to
<m>\overrightarrow{r}</m> then <m>\overrightarrow{\tau} = \overrightarrow{r} \times \overrightarrow{F} = \overrightarrow{0}</m>: a
force directed straight at (or away from) the axis produces no rotation.
This is why you cannot open a door by pushing toward its hinge.
</p>
</statement>
</fact>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-biceps-torque">
<title>Biceps torque</title>
<statement>
<p>
The biceps muscle exerts a vertical force of
<m>\|\overrightarrow{F}\| = 700</m> N on the lower arm, attached at a point
<m>5.0</m> cm from the elbow joint (the axis of rotation). Calculate
the torque about the elbow when
</p>
<p>
<ol>
<li><p>the forearm is horizontal, and</p></li>
<li><p>the forearm makes a <m>30^\circ</m> angle below the horizontal.</p></li>
</ol>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-biceps-torque">
  <caption>The biceps exerts a 700 N force on the forearm: (a) forearm horizontal, with the muscle attached 5.0 cm from the elbow axis; (b) forearm at 30<degree/> below horizontal, with lever arm <m>R</m>.</caption>
  <image source="images/biceps-side-by-side.png" width="100%">
    <description>
<p>
Side-by-side diagrams of an arm. In (a), the forearm is horizontal and a 700 N biceps force acts 5.0 cm from the elbow axis. In (b), the forearm is angled 30 degrees below horizontal, making a 60 degree angle with the vertical, with lever arm R.
</p>
</description>
  </image>
</figure>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Put the elbow at the origin and keep everything in the plane <m>z = 0</m>, so
<m>\overrightarrow F</m> points straight up and only <m>\overrightarrow r</m>
changes between the two parts. Convert the centimeters to meters before you
start. In part 2 the forearm sits at <m>-30^\circ</m>, so the angle between
<m>\overrightarrow r</m> and <m>\overrightarrow F</m> is not <m>30^\circ</m>
<mdash/> read it off the picture. Compute each torque with
<xref ref="eq-skel-torque-def"/> and check it against
<xref ref="eq-skel-torque-magnitude"/>. Finish by saying which position
produces the larger torque and why a gym machine might care.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,16);
\draw[gray!35] (0,13) -- (15,13);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7.5) -- (15,7.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,15.7) {Setup: $\overrightarrow{F} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.7) {1. Forearm horizontal: $\overrightarrow{r} =$, \quad $\overrightarrow{\tau} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.2) {2. Forearm $30^{\circ}$ below: $\overrightarrow{r} =$, \quad $\overrightarrow{\tau} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {Which is larger, and why it matters:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the force vector, for the position vector and torque with the forearm horizontal, for the same with the forearm thirty degrees below horizontal, and for the comparison of the two.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-biceps-torque-video">
<caption>
The torque <m>\overrightarrow{\tau} = \overrightarrow{r} \times \overrightarrow{F}</m> of the biceps force
about the elbow. When the forearm drops <m>30^\circ</m> below the
horizontal, the angle between <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> becomes
<m>120^\circ</m> and the lever arm <m>r_\perp = \|\overrightarrow{r}\|\sin\theta</m>
shortens.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-biceps-torque"
       source="videos/biceps_torque_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-circular-motion">
<title>Application: Circular Motion: Velocity and Angular Velocity</title>

<introduction>
<p>
A second physical application of the cross product describes the motion of
an object travelling in a circle: a rider on a circular track, a ball whirled
on a string, a point on a spinning wheel, or the moon on its (nearly
circular) orbit. In each case the object turns about a fixed centre, and the
natural way to keep track of where it is at time <m>t</m> is not by its
coordinates <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> but by the single angle <m>\theta(t)</m>
it has swung through. Our goal is to study how the velocity and the rate of
change of that angle are related.
</p>
</introduction>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-skel-polar-frame">
<title>A Frame that Turns with the Particle</title>

<p>
Let an object move on a circle of radius <m>r</m> centred at the origin of
the <m>xy</m>-plane, and let <m>\theta(t)</m> be the angle its position
makes with the positive <m>x</m>-axis at time <m>t</m>, as in
<xref ref="fig-skel-polar-frame"/>. At the point <m>P</m> where the particle
sits we attach two unit vectors:
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<m>\hat r(t)</m>, pointing radially outward, away from the centre, and
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\hat\theta(t)</m>, perpendicular to <m>\hat r(t)</m> and tangent to the
circle, pointing in the direction of increasing <m>\theta</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-polar-frame">
<caption>
The moving frame at the point <m>P</m>: <m>\hat r(t)</m> points radially
outward and <m>\hat\theta(t)</m> is tangent to the circle. The fixed vectors
<m>\hat{\imath}</m> and <m>\hat{\jmath}</m> are drawn at <m>P</m> for comparison; unlike
<m>\hat r</m> and <m>\hat\theta</m>, they do not turn as the particle moves.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<description>
<p>
A circle of radius r centred at the origin. A particle sits at a point P on
the circle, its position vector making an angle theta of t with the positive
x-axis. At P the unit vector r hat points radially outward along the
position vector, and the unit vector theta hat is perpendicular to it,
tangent to the circle in the counterclockwise direction. The fixed unit
vectors i and j are also drawn at P for comparison.
</p>
</description>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-polar-frame">
      <diagram dimensions="(455,430)" margins="14">
      <coordinates bbox="(-3.5,-3.1,3.9,3.9)">

      <line at="pf-ax" p1="(-3.15,0)" p2="(3.35,0)" stroke="#999999" thickness="1.4"/>
      <line at="pf-ay" p1="(0,-2.75)" p2="(0,3.35)" stroke="#999999" thickness="1.4"/>

      <circle at="pf-circle" center="(0,0)" radius="2.4"
              stroke="#9EC5E8" thickness="2" fill="none"/>

      <vector at="pf-r" tail="(0,0)" v="(1.5427,1.8384)" stroke="#666666" thickness="2"/>

      <vector at="pf-i" tail="(1.5427,1.8384)" v="(1,0)" stroke="#9EC5E8" thickness="2"/>
      <vector at="pf-j" tail="(1.5427,1.8384)" v="(0,1)" stroke="#9EC5E8" thickness="2"/>

      <vector at="pf-rhat" tail="(1.5427,1.8384)" v="(0.6428,0.766)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
      <vector at="pf-thetahat" tail="(1.5427,1.8384)" v="(-0.766,0.6428)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>

      <angle-marker font-size="18" at="pf-theta" points="((1,0),(0,0),(0.6428,0.766))"
                    radius="42" stroke="black"><m>\theta(t)</m></angle-marker>

      <point at="pf-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" radius="3"/>
      <point at="pf-P" p="(1.5427,1.8384)" fill="black" radius="3.5"/>

      <label font-size="18" at="pf-Pl" anchor="(1.60,1.76)" alignment="se"><m>P</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="pf-rl" anchor="(0.66,1.00)" alignment="nw" color="#666666"><m>r</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="pf-rhl" anchor="(2.24,2.66)" alignment="ne" color="#D55E00">
      <m>\hat r(t)</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="pf-thl" anchor="(0.72,2.54)" alignment="nw" color="#009E73">
      <m>\hat\theta(t)</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="pf-il" anchor="(2.60,1.80)" alignment="se" color="#5B9BD5"><m>\hat{\imath}</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="pf-jl" anchor="(1.60,2.88)" alignment="e" color="#5B9BD5"><m>\hat{\jmath}</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="pf-xl" anchor="(3.25,0.05)" alignment="ne"><m>+x</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="pf-yl" anchor="(0.10,3.25)" alignment="e"><m>+y</m></label>

      </coordinates>

      <annotations>
      <annotation ref="figure" text="A circle of radius r centred at the origin. A particle sits at a point P on the circle, its position vector making an angle theta of t with the positive x axis. At P the unit vector r hat points radially outward along the position vector and the unit vector theta hat is perpendicular to it, tangent to the circle in the counterclockwise direction. The fixed unit vectors i and j are also drawn at P for comparison.">
        <annotation ref="pf-circle" text="The circular orbit of radius r."/>
        <annotation ref="pf-r" text="The position vector of the particle, of length r."/>
        <annotation ref="pf-rhat" text="The unit vector r hat, pointing radially outward from the centre."/>
        <annotation ref="pf-thetahat" text="The unit vector theta hat, tangent to the circle, perpendicular to r hat."/>
        <annotation ref="pf-theta" text="The angle theta of t between the positive x axis and the position vector."/>
      </annotation>
      </annotations>
      </diagram>
      </prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Read the components of the moving frame off
<xref ref="fig-skel-polar-frame"/>, and write down the position vector.
(<m>\hat r</m> makes the angle <m>\theta</m> with the <m>x</m>-axis, and
<m>\hat\theta</m> is a quarter turn counterclockwise from it. Then check that
<m>\hat r</m>, <m>\hat\theta</m>, <m>\hat k</m> is right-handed by computing
<m>\hat r \times \hat\theta</m> with
<xref ref="eq-skel-cross-determinant"/>; a Pythagorean identity finishes it.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10.5) -- (15,10.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,8) -- (15,8);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.7) {$\hat r(t) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.2) {$\hat\theta(t) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {$\overrightarrow{r}(t) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (5.00,7.7) {$\hat r \times \hat\theta =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the components of the radial unit vector, for the components of the tangential unit vector, and for the position vector together with the cross product that checks the frame is right-handed.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-polar-frame">
<title>The Moving Frame</title>
<p>
<md>
<mrow xml:id="eq-skel-rhat" number="yes">\hat r(t) \amp = \cos\theta(t)\,\hat{\imath} + \sin\theta(t)\,\hat{\jmath},</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-skel-thetahat" number="yes">\hat\theta(t) \amp = -\sin\theta(t)\,\hat{\imath} + \cos\theta(t)\,\hat{\jmath},</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-skel-position-polar" number="yes">\overrightarrow{r}(t) \amp = r\,\hat r(t).</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<p>
The pair <m>\hat{\imath}</m>, <m>\hat{\jmath}</m> is fixed once and for all, while the
pair <m>\hat r(t)</m>, <m>\hat\theta(t)</m> rides along with the particle and
turns as it goes; this is exactly what makes the second pair convenient.
</p>

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-skel-circular-velocity">
<title>The Velocity in Circular Motion</title>

<p>
Because the particle moves, the angle <m>\theta</m> depends on <m>t</m>, and
so do the two moving unit vectors. Differentiate the frame, and then the
position. (Differentiate <xref ref="eq-skel-rhat"/> and
<xref ref="eq-skel-thetahat"/> with the chain rule, and recognize each answer
as a multiple of the <em>other</em> unit vector. Then differentiate
<xref ref="eq-skel-position-polar"/>, remembering that <m>r</m> is constant on
a circle. Finish by saying which of <m>\hat r</m>, <m>\hat\theta</m> the
velocity points along, and why that had to happen for a vector of constant
length.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,15);
\draw[gray!35] (0,11.5) -- (15,11.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,8) -- (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,14.7) {$\dfrac{d\hat r}{dt} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.2) {$\dfrac{d\hat\theta}{dt} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {$\overrightarrow{v}(t) = \dfrac{d\overrightarrow{r}}{dt} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {Why the velocity has no radial component:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the derivative of the radial unit vector, for the derivative of the tangential unit vector, for the velocity, and for the explanation that the velocity is purely tangential.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-circular-velocity">
<title>Velocity in Circular Motion</title>
<p>
<md xml:id="eq-skel-circular-velocity" number="yes">
\overrightarrow{v}(t) = r\,\frac{d\theta}{dt}\,\hat\theta(t),
</md>
whose <m>\hat\theta</m>-component
<md xml:id="eq-skel-tangential-velocity" number="yes">
v_\theta = r\,\frac{d\theta}{dt}
</md>
is called the <term>tangential component of the velocity</term>. It is
positive when the particle turns counterclockwise and negative when it turns
clockwise.
</p>
</assemblage>

<definition xml:id="def-skel-angular-speed">
<title>Angular Speed</title>
<statement>
<p>
The <term>angular speed</term> of the particle is the magnitude of the rate
of change of its angle,
<md xml:id="eq-skel-angular-speed" number="yes">
\omega = \left|\frac{d\theta}{dt}\right|,
</md>
measured in radians per second. By <xref ref="eq-skel-circular-velocity"/>,
the speed of the particle and its angular speed are related by
<md xml:id="eq-skel-speed-omega-r" number="yes">
v = \left\|\overrightarrow{v}\right\| = r\,\omega.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
It is worth seeing where <xref ref="eq-skel-speed-omega-r"/> comes from
geometrically, without any differentiation of unit vectors.
<xref ref="fig-skel-circular-chord"/> shows the particle moving from
<m>\overrightarrow{r}(t)</m> to <m>\overrightarrow{r}(t+\Delta t)</m> through a small angle
<m>\Delta\theta</m>. Recover <xref ref="eq-skel-speed-omega-r"/> from that
picture. (The two position vectors have the same length, so the triangle is
isosceles; splitting it down the middle gives the chord length. For small
angles replace <m>\sin x</m> by the first term of its Maclaurin series, then
divide by <m>\Delta t</m> and let <m>\Delta t \to 0</m>. Say also what happens
to the <em>direction</em> of the chord in that limit.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10) -- (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.7) {Chord length: $\|\Delta\overrightarrow{r}\| =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.7) {Taking the limit:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {The direction of the chord as $\Delta t \to 0$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the exact chord length, for the limit that produces the speed, and for the limiting direction of the chord.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-circular-chord">
<caption>
The displacement <m>\Delta\overrightarrow{r}</m> is the chord subtending the angle
<m>\Delta\theta</m>. For small <m>\Delta\theta</m> the chord is nearly the
arc, <m>\left\|\Delta\overrightarrow{r}\right\| \approx r\,\Delta\theta</m>, and its
direction approaches that of the tangent line, along which <m>\overrightarrow{v}</m>
points.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<description>
<p>
Two position vectors of equal length r are drawn from the centre of a dashed
circle, separated by a small angle delta theta. The chord delta r joins the
tip of the first to the tip of the second. A dashed tangent line touches the
circle at the first point, and the velocity arrow at that point lies along
it.
</p>
</description>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-circular-chord">
      <diagram dimensions="(470,492)" margins="14">
      <coordinates bbox="(-3.7,-4.25,3.7,3.5)">

      <circle at="ch-circle" center="(0,0)" radius="2.2"
              stroke="#9EC5E8" thickness="1.8" fill="none" dash="4 3"/>

      <line at="ch-tangent" p1="(1.7062,1.5553)" p2="(-0.8052,3.0053)"
            stroke="#999999" thickness="1.4" dash="4 3"/>

      <vector at="ch-r1" tail="(0,0)" v="(1.1000,1.9053)" stroke="#666666" thickness="2"/>
      <vector at="ch-r2" tail="(0,0)" v="(-0.3820,2.1666)" stroke="#666666" thickness="2"/>

      <vector at="ch-dr" tail="(1.1000,1.9053)" v="(-1.4820,0.2613)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
      <vector at="ch-v" tail="(1.1000,1.9053)" v="(-1.2990,0.7500)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>

      <angle-marker font-size="18" at="ch-dtheta" points="((0.5,0.8660),(0,0),(-0.1736,0.9848))"
                    radius="52" stroke="black"><m>\Delta\theta</m></angle-marker>

      <point at="ch-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" radius="3"/>
      <point at="ch-P1" p="(1.1000,1.9053)" fill="black" radius="3.5"/>
      <point at="ch-P2" p="(-0.3820,2.1666)" fill="black" radius="3.5"/>

      <label font-size="18" at="ch-r1l" anchor="(1.32,1.98)" alignment="e" color="#666666">
      <m>\overrightarrow{r}(t)</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="ch-r2l" anchor="(-0.55,2.24)" alignment="nw" color="#666666">
      <m>\overrightarrow{r}(t+\Delta t)</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="ch-drl" anchor="(0.30,1.92)" alignment="s" color="#D55E00">
      <m>\Delta\overrightarrow{r}</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="ch-vl" anchor="(-0.10,2.70)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
      <m>\overrightarrow{v}(t)</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="ch-tl" anchor="(-0.86,3.06)" alignment="nw" color="#666666">
      <m>\text{tangent line}</m>
      </label>

      <!-- Plain \| bars, never \left\|...\right\|: a stretchy delimiter makes
           MathJax emit a nested <svg> inside the label, which browsers render
           flipped and stacked at the diagram origin. -->
      <label font-size="18" at="ch-note1" anchor="(0,-2.95)" alignment="c">
      <m>\|\Delta\overrightarrow{r}\| = 2r\sin\tfrac{\Delta\theta}{2} \approx r\,\Delta\theta</m>
      </label>

      </coordinates>

      <annotations>
      <annotation ref="figure" text="Two position vectors of equal length r are drawn from the centre of a dashed circle, separated by a small angle delta theta. The chord delta r joins the tip of the first to the tip of the second. A dashed tangent line touches the circle at the first point, and the velocity arrow at that point lies along it. As delta t tends to zero the chord lines up with the tangent, and its length, two r sine of half delta theta, is approximately r times delta theta.">
        <annotation ref="ch-r1" text="The position vector at time t."/>
        <annotation ref="ch-r2" text="The position vector a short time delta t later, the same length but turned through delta theta."/>
        <annotation ref="ch-dr" text="The displacement delta r, the chord joining the two tips."/>
        <annotation ref="ch-v" text="The velocity at time t, tangent to the circle."/>
        <annotation ref="ch-tangent" text="The tangent line to the circle at the position at time t."/>
        <annotation ref="ch-dtheta" text="The small angle delta theta swept out during the time interval."/>
      </annotation>
      </annotations>
      </diagram>
      </prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-skel-angular-velocity-vector">
<title>Angular Velocity as a Vector</title>

<p>
So far the rate of turning has been a number, <m>d\theta/dt</m>. But a
rotation has a direction as well as a rate: the plane of the circle can be
tilted any which way, and the particle can go around it either way. Both
pieces of information can be carried by a single vector, provided we point it
along the axis of rotation rather than along the motion.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-skel-angular-velocity">
<title>Angular Velocity Vector</title>
<statement>
<p>
For an object moving in a circle in the <m>xy</m>-plane about the origin, the
<term>angular velocity vector</term> is
<md xml:id="eq-skel-angular-velocity" number="yes">
\overrightarrow{\omega} = \frac{d\theta}{dt}\,\hat{k} = \omega_z\,\hat{k},
</md>
directed along the axis of rotation. Its SI units are
<m>\text{rad}\cdot\text{s}^{-1}</m>, and the angular speed of
<xref ref="eq-skel-angular-speed"/> is its magnitude,
<m>\omega = \left\|\overrightarrow{\omega}\right\| = \left|\omega_z\right|</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
The sign of <m>\omega_z</m> records which way the object goes around, and the
right-hand rule turns that sign into a direction in space: curl the fingers
of your right hand in the direction of the motion and your thumb points along
<m>\overrightarrow{\omega}</m>. The two cases are shown in
<xref ref="fig-skel-angular-velocity-sign"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-angular-velocity-sign">
<caption>
The angular velocity vector points along the axis of rotation, in the
direction given by the right-hand rule: up the <m>z</m>-axis for
counterclockwise motion (left), down it for clockwise motion (right). Note
that <m>\overrightarrow{\omega}</m> is <em>not</em> tangent to the orbit <mdash/> the
velocity <m>\overrightarrow{v}</m> is.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<description>
<p>
Two panels showing a particle on a circular orbit in the horizontal plane,
drawn in an oblique view with the z-axis vertical. In the left panel the
particle circulates counterclockwise as seen from above and the angular
velocity vector points up along the positive z-direction. In the right panel
the particle circulates clockwise and the angular velocity vector points down
along the negative z-direction. In both panels the velocity is tangent to the
orbit.
</p>
</description>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-angular-velocity-sign">
      <diagram dimensions="(710,372)" margins="14">
      <definition>orbA(t) = (2.2*cos(t), 0.75*sin(t))</definition>
      <definition>orbB(t) = (7.4 + 2.2*cos(t), 0.75*sin(t))</definition>
      <coordinates bbox="(-3.5,-3.7,11.2,4.0)">

      <!-- ============ PANEL A : counterclockwise, omega along +k ============ -->
      <line at="av-axA" p1="(0,-2.7)" p2="(0,3.05)" stroke="#999999" thickness="1.4"/>
      <parametric-curve at="av-orbA" function="orbA" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                        stroke="#9EC5E8" thickness="2"/>
      <parametric-curve at="av-dirA" function="orbA" domain="(2.0,2.9)"
                        stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
      <vector at="av-rA" tail="(0,0)" v="(1.8021,-0.4302)" stroke="#666666" thickness="2"/>
      <vector at="av-vA" tail="(1.8021,-0.4302)" v="(1.1237,0.5471)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
      <vector at="av-wA" tail="(0,0)" v="(0,2.5)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="4"/>
      <point at="av-OA" p="(0,0)" fill="black" radius="3"/>
      <point at="av-PA" p="(1.8021,-0.4302)" fill="black" radius="3.5"/>

      <label font-size="18" at="av-wAl" anchor="(0.14,2.42)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
      <m>\overrightarrow{\omega}</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="av-zAl" anchor="(0.10,3.05)" alignment="e"><m>+z</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="av-OAl" anchor="(-0.12,-0.10)" alignment="sw"><m>O</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="av-rAl" anchor="(0.98,-0.34)" alignment="s" color="#666666">
      <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="av-vAl" anchor="(3.00,0.18)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
      <m>\overrightarrow{v}</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="av-topA" anchor="(-3.35,3.60)" alignment="e">
      <m>\omega_z = \dfrac{d\theta}{dt} > 0</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="av-botA" anchor="(-3.35,-3.25)" alignment="e">
      <m>\text{counterclockwise:}\;\; \overrightarrow{\omega} \text{ points along } +\hat{k}</m>
      </label>

      <!-- ============ PANEL B : clockwise, omega along -k ============ -->
      <line at="av-axB" p1="(7.4,-2.7)" p2="(7.4,3.05)" stroke="#999999" thickness="1.4"/>
      <parametric-curve at="av-orbB" function="orbB" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                        stroke="#9EC5E8" thickness="2"/>
      <parametric-curve at="av-dirB" function="orbB" domain="(2.9,2.0)"
                        stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
      <vector at="av-rB" tail="(7.4,0)" v="(1.8021,-0.4302)" stroke="#666666" thickness="2"/>
      <vector at="av-vB" tail="(9.2021,-0.4302)" v="(-1.1237,-0.5471)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
      <vector at="av-wB" tail="(7.4,0)" v="(0,-2.5)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="4"/>
      <point at="av-OB" p="(7.4,0)" fill="black" radius="3"/>
      <point at="av-PB" p="(9.2021,-0.4302)" fill="black" radius="3.5"/>

      <label font-size="18" at="av-wBl" anchor="(7.54,-2.42)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
      <m>\overrightarrow{\omega}</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="av-zBl" anchor="(7.50,3.05)" alignment="e"><m>+z</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="av-OBl" anchor="(7.28,-0.10)" alignment="sw"><m>O</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="av-rBl" anchor="(8.34,-0.08)" alignment="n" color="#666666">
      <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="av-vBl" anchor="(8.02,-1.06)" alignment="sw" color="#0072B2">
      <m>\overrightarrow{v}</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="av-topB" anchor="(4.45,3.60)" alignment="e">
      <m>\omega_z = \dfrac{d\theta}{dt} &lt; 0</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="av-botB" anchor="(4.45,-3.25)" alignment="e">
      <m>\text{clockwise:}\;\; \overrightarrow{\omega} \text{ points along } -\hat{k}</m>
      </label>

      </coordinates>

      <annotations>
      <annotation ref="figure" text="Two panels showing a particle on a circular orbit in the horizontal plane, drawn in an oblique view with the z axis vertical. In the left panel the particle circulates counterclockwise as seen from above, the rate of change of theta is positive, and the angular velocity vector points up along the positive z direction. In the right panel the particle circulates clockwise, the rate of change of theta is negative, and the angular velocity vector points down along the negative z direction. In both panels the velocity is tangent to the orbit and the position vector runs from the centre out to the particle.">
        <annotation ref="av-orbA" text="The circular orbit in the left panel."/>
        <annotation ref="av-dirA" text="An arrowhead on the orbit showing counterclockwise circulation."/>
        <annotation ref="av-wA" text="The angular velocity vector pointing up along the positive z axis."/>
        <annotation ref="av-vA" text="The velocity of the particle, tangent to the orbit."/>
        <annotation ref="av-orbB" text="The circular orbit in the right panel."/>
        <annotation ref="av-dirB" text="An arrowhead on the orbit showing clockwise circulation."/>
        <annotation ref="av-wB" text="The angular velocity vector pointing down along the negative z axis."/>
        <annotation ref="av-vB" text="The velocity of the particle, tangent to the orbit."/>
      </annotation>
      </annotations>
      </diagram>
      </prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-skel-v-omega-cross-r">
<title>Velocity as a Cross Product</title>

<p>
We now have two vectors attached to the motion: the angular velocity
<m>\overrightarrow{\omega}</m>, along the axis, and the position <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m>, in the
plane of the circle. The velocity is perpendicular to both, which is precisely
the job of the cross product.
</p>

<fact xml:id="fact-skel-velocity-cross-product">
<title>Velocity and Angular Velocity</title>
<statement>
<p>
For an object in circular motion about the origin,
<md xml:id="eq-skel-v-omega-cross-r" number="yes">
\overrightarrow{v} = \overrightarrow{\omega} \times \overrightarrow{r}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</fact>

<p>
Verify <xref ref="eq-skel-v-omega-cross-r"/>. (Start by computing
<m>\hat{k} \times \hat r</m> from <xref ref="eq-skel-rhat"/> with the
determinant; the answer is one of the two frame vectors. Then substitute
<xref ref="eq-skel-angular-velocity"/> and
<xref ref="eq-skel-position-polar"/>, pull the scalars out with property 1 of
<xref ref="thm-skel-cross-properties"/>, and compare with
<xref ref="eq-skel-circular-velocity"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6.5) -- (15,6.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.7) {$\hat{k}\times\hat r =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.2) {$\overrightarrow{\omega}\times\overrightarrow{r} =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the cross product of k hat with r hat and one for the verification that omega cross r is the velocity.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
The geometry of the cross product delivers all three features of the velocity
at once. Fill them in from <xref ref="fig-skel-omega-cross-r"/>, with no
computation. (For the magnitude, use the angle between <m>\overrightarrow\omega</m>
and <m>\overrightarrow r</m> in <xref ref="eq-skel-cross-geometric"/>. For the
direction, use that a cross product is perpendicular to both of its factors.
For the sense, ask what happens to <m>\overrightarrow\omega \times \overrightarrow r</m>
when <m>\overrightarrow\omega</m> is reversed.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6.7) -- (15,6.7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.4) -- (15,3.4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.7) {Magnitude:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.4) {Direction:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.1) {Sense:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three labelled rows, for the magnitude, the direction, and the sense of the velocity read off the cross product.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
There is a bonus. A piece of <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m> running along the axis is parallel
to <m>\overrightarrow{\omega}</m> and so contributes nothing to the cross product, since
<m>\hat{k} \times \left(z\,\hat{k}\right) = \overrightarrow{0}</m>. Consequently
<xref ref="eq-skel-v-omega-cross-r"/> remains true when <m>\overrightarrow{r}</m> is
measured from <em>any</em> point on the axis of rotation, not just from the
centre of the circle.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-omega-cross-r">
<caption>
The velocity of a particle in circular motion is
<m>\overrightarrow{v} = \overrightarrow{\omega} \times \overrightarrow{r}</m>. Because <m>\overrightarrow{\omega}</m> and
<m>\overrightarrow{r}</m> are perpendicular, the speed is <m>\omega r</m>, and the
right-hand rule places <m>\overrightarrow{v}</m> tangent to the orbit in the direction of
travel.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<description>
<p>
A circular orbit in the horizontal plane drawn in an oblique view, with the
z-axis vertical. The angular velocity vector points straight up along the
z-axis from the centre O. The position vector runs from O out to the particle
on the orbit, and the velocity vector at the particle is tangent to the
orbit, equal to the cross product of the angular velocity with the position
vector.
</p>
</description>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-omega-cross-r">
      <diagram dimensions="(500,450)" margins="14">
      <definition>orb(t) = (2.6*cos(t), 0.9*sin(t))</definition>
      <coordinates bbox="(-4.8,-4.6,5.0,4.2)">

      <line at="cr-ax" p1="(0,-2.6)" p2="(0,3.5)" stroke="#999999" thickness="1.4"/>
      <parametric-curve at="cr-orb" function="orb" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                        stroke="#9EC5E8" thickness="2"/>
      <parametric-curve at="cr-dir" function="orb" domain="(2.0,2.9)"
                        stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>

      <vector at="cr-r" tail="(0,0)" v="(2.2517,-0.45)" stroke="#666666" thickness="2"/>
      <vector at="cr-v" tail="(2.2517,-0.45)" v="(1.4581,0.8742)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
      <vector at="cr-w" tail="(0,0)" v="(0,2.9)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="4"/>

      <point at="cr-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" radius="3"/>
      <point at="cr-P" p="(2.2517,-0.45)" fill="black" radius="4"/>

      <label font-size="18" at="cr-wl" anchor="(0.16,2.55)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
      <m>\overrightarrow{\omega} = \dfrac{d\theta}{dt}\,\hat{k}</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="cr-zl" anchor="(0.10,3.50)" alignment="e"><m>+z</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="cr-Ol" anchor="(-0.12,-0.10)" alignment="sw"><m>O</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="cr-rl" anchor="(1.20,-0.34)" alignment="s" color="#666666">
      <m>\overrightarrow{r} = r\,\hat r</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="cr-vl" anchor="(3.78,0.48)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
      <m>\overrightarrow{v} = \overrightarrow{\omega}\times\overrightarrow{r}</m>
      </label>

      </coordinates>

      <annotations>
      <annotation ref="figure" text="A circular orbit in the horizontal plane drawn in an oblique view, with the z axis vertical. The angular velocity vector points straight up along the z axis from the centre O. The position vector runs from O out to the particle on the orbit, and the velocity vector at the particle is tangent to the orbit. The velocity is the cross product of the angular velocity with the position vector; since the two factors are perpendicular, the speed is omega times r.">
        <annotation ref="cr-orb" text="The circular orbit of the particle."/>
        <annotation ref="cr-dir" text="An arrowhead on the orbit showing the sense of circulation."/>
        <annotation ref="cr-w" text="The angular velocity vector, pointing up the z axis, perpendicular to the plane of the orbit."/>
        <annotation ref="cr-r" text="The position vector from the centre out to the particle, equal to r times r hat."/>
        <annotation ref="cr-v" text="The velocity, equal to the cross product of the angular velocity with the position vector, tangent to the orbit."/>
      </annotation>
      </annotations>
      </diagram>
      </prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-skel-circular-motion-examples">
<title>Examples</title>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-angular-velocity">
<title>Angular velocity from an angle function</title>
<statement>
<p>
A particle moves in a circle of radius <m>R</m> centred at the origin of the
<m>xy</m>-plane; at <m>t = 0</m> it is on the positive <m>x</m>-axis. The
angle it makes with the positive <m>x</m>-axis is
<m>\theta(t) = At - Bt^3</m>, where <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are positive
constants.
</p>
<p>
<ol>
<li><p>Find the angular velocity vector.</p></li>
<li><p>Find the velocity vector, in polar coordinates.</p></li>
<li><p>At what time <m>t_1</m> is the angular velocity zero?</p></li>
<li>
<p>
In which direction does the angular velocity point for <m>t &lt; t_1</m>,
and for <m>t > t_1</m>?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Everything follows from <m>d\theta/dt</m>, so compute that first, then apply
<xref ref="eq-skel-angular-velocity"/> and
<xref ref="eq-skel-circular-velocity"/>. Part 3 is one quadratic equation; keep
only the positive root and say what the particle is doing at that instant.
For part 4, the direction is decided by the <em>sign</em> of
<m>d\theta/dt</m>, not its size.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,11.5) -- (15,11.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9) -- (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.7) {$\dfrac{d\theta}{dt} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.2) {1. $\overrightarrow{\omega}(t) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {2. $\overrightarrow{v}(t) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {3. $t_1 =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {4. Before $t_1$: \qquad\qquad After $t_1$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in five rows, for the rate of change of the angle, the angular velocity vector, the velocity vector, the time at which the angular velocity vanishes, and the direction before and after that time.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-turntable">
<title>A turntable</title>
<statement>
<p>
A record turntable spins at <m>33\tfrac13</m> revolutions per minute,
counterclockwise when viewed from above. Take the axis of rotation to be the
<m>z</m>-axis, with the turntable lying in the <m>xy</m>-plane, distances in
meters.
</p>
<p>
<ol>
<li><p>Find the angular velocity vector <m>\overrightarrow{\omega}</m>.</p></li>
<li>
<p>
Find the velocity of the point of the record at
<m>\overrightarrow{r} = \left\langle 0.12,\,0,\,0 \right\rangle</m>, and its speed.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Convert revolutions per minute to radians per second before anything else;
one revolution is <m>2\pi</m> radians. The sense of rotation fixes the sign of
<m>\omega_z</m>. Then use <xref ref="eq-skel-v-omega-cross-r"/>, and check the
speed against <xref ref="eq-skel-speed-omega-r"/>. Confirm that the direction
you get really is counterclockwise for a point on the positive
<m>x</m>-axis.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9.5) -- (15,9.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.7) {1. $\omega =$ \qquad\qquad $\overrightarrow{\omega} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.2) {2. $\overrightarrow{v} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {Speed, and the check:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the angular speed and angular velocity vector, for the velocity of the point, and for the speed together with its check.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<exercise xml:id="skel-exercise-carousel-velocity">
<title>A carousel</title>
<statement>
<p>
A carousel turns <em>clockwise</em> when viewed from above, making one
complete turn every <m>12</m> seconds. Taking the axis of rotation as the
<m>z</m>-axis, find the angular velocity vector, and the velocity of a horse
located at <m>\overrightarrow{r} = \left\langle 0,\,4,\,0\right\rangle</m> meters.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(The period is given rather than the frequency, so <m>\omega = 2\pi/T</m>.
Clockwise from above settles the sign, as in
<xref ref="fig-skel-angular-velocity-sign"/>. Then apply
<xref ref="eq-skel-v-omega-cross-r"/>, and check that the direction you get for
a horse on the positive <m>y</m>-axis really is clockwise.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,8.5) -- (15,8.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.7) {$\omega =$ \qquad\qquad $\overrightarrow{\omega} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.2) {$\overrightarrow{v} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {Direction check:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the angular speed and angular velocity vector, for the velocity of the horse, and for the check on its direction.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</exercise>

</subsubsection>

</subsection>

</section>
